// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 4.0 Security Operations (part A: 4.1-4.4)
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 4.1 Secure baselines & hardening (18) ----------
{o:"4.1",q:"A company documents the required security settings for every new Windows server and compares each deployed server against that document every month. What is the document of required settings called?",c:["Vulnerability assessment report","Risk register entry for server risks","Secure baseline document","Change advisory board record"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An assessment report lists weaknesses found by scanning; it is not the target configuration. It would fit if the stem described scan findings awaiting remediation.","Incorrect. A risk register tracks identified risks, owners and treatments, not required system settings. It would fit a question about documenting and tracking a specific risk.","Correct. A secure baseline is the approved, documented set of security settings that systems are deployed with and then measured against to detect drift.","Incorrect. A CAB record documents approval of a proposed change. It would fit if the servers were being modified, not compared against a standing configuration."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"The secure baseline lifecycle begins with establishing a baseline and deploying it to systems. Which step follows deployment?",c:["Maintain: monitor for drift and update as threats and software change","Recover: restore systems to a known-good state after an incident","Eradicate: remove malicious artifacts from compromised systems","Contain: isolate and quarantine systems that fall outside the baseline"],a:0,e:["Correct. Maintenance keeps the baseline current by detecting drift and revising settings after new software versions, vulnerabilities and threats appear.","Incorrect. Recovery is an incident response phase that follows containment and eradication. It would fit if systems had been compromised, not routine lifecycle upkeep.","Incorrect. Eradication is an incident response step for removing malware or attacker access. It would be right if an infection had been confirmed on the deployed servers.","Incorrect. Containment is an incident response action to limit active damage. It might be used on a compromised host, but it is not the planned lifecycle step after deployment."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Which activity is the foundation of hardening the operating system of a newly built, internet-facing web server?",c:["Deploy a WAF in front of the server and tune its rule set","Forward server logs to the SIEM and build correlation alerts","Schedule weekly vulnerability scans and track the findings","Remove unneeded services and software, then patch the rest"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A WAF is a valuable network-layer control for web attacks, but it does not harden the server's own configuration. It would fit a question about filtering HTTP exploits.","Incorrect. Logging and alerting are detective controls that reveal attacks; they do not reduce the attack surface. They fit a question about monitoring, not configuration hardening.","Incorrect. Scanning identifies weaknesses but does not itself remove any. It fits assessment goals, whereas hardening means actually reducing services and applying fixes.","Correct. Shrinking the attack surface and patching the remaining components is the core of OS hardening, and everything else builds on it."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"An employee's company phone, which holds cached customer records, was left on a train and may be in a stranger's hands. Recovering the phone is unimportant; the data must not be exposed. Which MDM capability addresses this?",c:["Geolocation tracking of the device","Remote wipe of the device","Geofencing around the office","Containerization of corporate apps"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Tracking helps find the phone, but the stem says recovery is unimportant. It would be right if the goal were retrieving a lost device.","Correct. Remote wipe erases the data even if the device is never recovered, which matches the stated goal of protecting data rather than finding the phone.","Incorrect. Geofencing triggers policy actions when a device crosses a boundary and does not erase data on its own. It fits restricting features to a physical area.","Incorrect. Containerization separates work data from personal data but does not remove it from a lost device by itself. It fits BYOD privacy separation."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Employees use their own phones for work. The company wants to manage and, if needed, erase only a work partition and never touch personal photos or messages. Which approach fits?",c:["BYOD with a managed work container","COPE with full management of the device","CYOD with full management of the device","COBO with full management of the device"],a:0,e:["Correct. Employee-owned devices with a managed work container let IT control and wipe corporate data only, preserving personal privacy.","Incorrect. COPE devices are company-owned and fully managed, so there is no personal ownership to protect. It fits when the company buys the phones.","Incorrect. CYOD means employees pick from approved company-owned models and IT manages the whole device. A full wipe would hit any personal data on it.","Incorrect. COBO means company-owned, business use only, so it does not involve personal phones. It fits strict environments that forbid personal use."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A company buys one standard phone model for all staff, fully manages the devices, and allows limited personal use. Which deployment model is this?",c:["BYOD (employees bring their own device)","CYOD (employee picks from approved list)","COBO (corporate-owned, business only)","COPE (corporate-owned, personal use)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. In BYOD the employee owns the phone. It would fit if staff supplied their own devices and IT managed only a work profile.","Incorrect. CYOD lets each employee pick from an approved list. Here the company chose a single model, so there is no employee choice.","Incorrect. COBO is company-owned but prohibits personal use. The stem explicitly allows limited personal use, which points to COPE instead.","Correct. The company owns and fully manages the device while permitting personal use, which is the definition of COPE."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A factory has legacy wireless sensors that only support WPA with TKIP. Replacement is not budgeted this year. Which approach is most appropriate?",c:["Move the sensors to WEP with a 128-bit key, since it is compatible with older hardware","Keep WPA-TKIP, isolating the legacy sensors on a dedicated VLAN until replaced","Require WPA2-AES-CCMP on the shared SSID so every device must upgrade or disconnect","Enable WPA3-SAE on the same SSID, since older clients negotiate it automatically"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. WEP is weaker than TKIP and trivially cracked. It is never an upgrade path; it is simply an older broken protocol.","Correct. TKIP was a stopgap for old hardware. If it cannot be retired yet, segmenting the devices limits the exposure of the weaker protocol.","Incorrect. This is the right end state, but these sensors cannot support AES, so they would lose connectivity. It fits once the hardware is replaced.","Incorrect. Clients must support SAE; hardware limited to TKIP cannot negotiate it. WPA3 fits environments where all devices have modern radios."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A small office uses a shared passphrase with no RADIUS server. The owner wants protection against offline dictionary attacks on a captured handshake, plus forward secrecy. Which setting fits?",c:["WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication","WPA2-Personal with AES-CCMP encryption","WPA3-Personal with a shared passphrase","WPA2-Enterprise with PEAP-MSCHAPv2"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Enterprise mode provides stronger per-user security but needs 802.1X and a RADIUS server, which the office does not have.","Incorrect. WPA2-PSK uses a handshake that can be captured and attacked offline, and it lacks forward secrecy. It fits when devices cannot support WPA3.","Correct. WPA3-Personal uses SAE in place of the PSK handshake, resisting offline dictionary attacks and providing forward secrecy with a shared passphrase.","Incorrect. This requires a RADIUS server and per-user credentials. It fits larger organizations with a directory, not a shared-passphrase office."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A company wants each Wi-Fi user to authenticate individually using directory credentials or certificates, with access revoked per user. Which configuration is required?",c:["WPA3-Personal with a per-department passphrase","Enhanced Open (OWE) with a captive portal","WPA3-Enterprise using 802.1X and RADIUS","WPA2-Personal with WPS PIN pairing enabled"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Shared passphrases identify a group, not an individual, so revoking one person means changing the passphrase for everyone in the group.","Incorrect. OWE encrypts open networks without authentication. A captive portal adds weak, web-based login but not per-user 802.1X identity.","Correct. 802.1X with RADIUS authenticates users individually against a directory or by certificate, enabling per-user revocation.","Incorrect. WPS PIN pairing is a convenience feature with known brute-force weaknesses and still uses one shared key for all users."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Before deploying wireless access points across a new office, the security team walks the building with a survey tool to produce a heat map. What is the main security goal?",c:["Find coverage gaps, interference and signal spill outside the building","Verify every deployed AP runs the latest vendor firmware release","Confirm every client authenticates with an 802.1X certificate","Measure the wired uplink throughput and latency to each access point"],a:0,e:["Correct. A site survey maps signal strength and interference and shows where coverage leaks beyond the premises, helping tune AP placement and power.","Incorrect. Firmware checks are a configuration management task. They fit patching reviews, not an RF walk-through.","Incorrect. Verifying authentication is a configuration test of the RADIUS and client setup. It does not need an RF heat map.","Incorrect. Uplink throughput is a network performance test. A site survey measures wireless signal and interference, not wired capacity."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"An auditor finds a core switch with Telnet enabled, default admin credentials, and unused ports left active. Which set of actions addresses these findings?",c:["Enable 802.1X on user ports, add DHCP snooping, and deploy dynamic ARP inspection","Enable BPDU guard, root guard and storm control on access ports","Configure VLAN segmentation, trunk pruning and a non-default native VLAN","Disable unused ports, change default credentials, and replace Telnet with SSH"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. These are strong layer 2 defenses against rogue devices and spoofing, but none addresses Telnet, default credentials or idle ports.","Incorrect. These protect spanning tree and prevent broadcast storms. They fit loop and topology attacks, not the management-plane findings here.","Incorrect. These limit VLAN hopping and traffic scope. They fit segmentation reviews, but they leave Telnet and default logins unchanged.","Correct. Each action maps directly to a finding: unused ports, default credentials and cleartext management access."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Which OS hardening step most reduces the damage when a user opens malware that runs in their session and tries to install services or change system settings?",c:["Enforce a password-protected screen saver timeout","Remove local administrator rights from standard users","Enable full-disk encryption backed by the TPM","Configure the host firewall to block unsolicited inbound connections"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A screen lock protects an unattended session from physical access. It does nothing about malware already running in the user's session.","Correct. Without admin rights, malware running as the user usually cannot install services, load drivers or alter system settings.","Incorrect. FDE protects data on a lost or stolen device, not against code executing on a running, unlocked system.","Incorrect. An inbound block helps against network attacks, but malware opened by the user initiates outbound traffic and still changes settings if privileged."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A plant uses legacy PLCs running proprietary firmware. They cannot run third-party software, and vendor patches require a production shutdown. Which hardening approach fits best?",c:["Segment them behind a firewall and monitor their traffic passively","Deploy EDR agents on each controller and enforce weekly automatic patching","Join the controllers to the domain and apply Group Policy baselines","Enable full-disk encryption and manage the controllers through MDM"],a:0,e:["Correct. Constrained devices that cannot host agents or be patched easily rely on network controls such as segmentation and passive monitoring.","Incorrect. EDR needs a supported OS and agents cannot be installed on proprietary firmware. Weekly patching conflicts with the shutdown requirement.","Incorrect. Embedded controllers do not run a domain-joinable OS, so Group Policy cannot be applied. It fits ordinary Windows endpoints.","Incorrect. PLCs have no disk encryption or MDM enrollment capability. These controls fit laptops and phones."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A cloud team uses CIS Benchmarks when configuring new instances. What are CIS Benchmarks?",c:["A standardized scoring system for vulnerability severity","A curated database of publicly disclosed vulnerabilities","A framework of attacker tactics and techniques","Consensus-based secure configuration guidelines"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. That describes CVSS, which rates how severe a vulnerability is rather than prescribing configuration settings.","Incorrect. That describes the NVD and CVE list, which catalog vulnerabilities rather than recommend configurations.","Incorrect. That describes MITRE ATT&CK, which models adversary behavior and does not give hardening settings.","Correct. CIS Benchmarks are community-developed hardening guides with recommended settings for operating systems, cloud platforms and applications."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A company must route traffic confidentially between three remote offices over the public internet while enforcing traffic filtering at each site edge. Which design fits?",c:["Layer 2 switches trunked across the ISP links using 802.1Q","Edge routers with ACLs and site-to-site IPsec tunnels","Web application firewalls with TLS offloading at each site","Forward proxies with URL filtering at each site"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Switches forward frames within a network and provide no encryption across the internet. VLAN trunking fits internal segmentation.","Correct. Routers connect sites, ACLs filter traffic, and IPsec tunnels encrypt it across the public internet.","Incorrect. A WAF protects web applications from HTTP attacks. It does not build encrypted site-to-site paths for all traffic.","Incorrect. Proxies control outbound web browsing and do not encrypt inter-site traffic. They fit acceptable-use enforcement."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Why should static and dynamic application security testing be performed on custom software before it is released?",c:["Passing both tests reduces the need to patch the underlying OS and libraries","Automated testing can substitute for manual code review and threat modeling","Defects caught before release are far cheaper to fix than those found in production","Dynamic testing finds the same flaws as static analysis, so running both is redundant"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Application testing covers your own code. The OS and third-party libraries still need patching on their own schedule.","Incorrect. Automated tools miss logic flaws and design weaknesses that reviews and threat modeling find, so they complement each other.","Correct. Early SAST and DAST findings can be corrected in development at a fraction of the cost of a production fix.","Incorrect. SAST examines code at rest and DAST tests the running app, so each finds issues the other can miss."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A laptop is stolen from a car and the attacker removes the drive to read it on another computer. Which hardening step best protects the data at rest?",c:["Always-on VPN client for corporate resources","Application allow listing on the endpoint","Full-disk encryption protected by the TPM","Host-based IPS running on the endpoint"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A VPN protects data in transit over networks, not files stored on a removed drive.","Incorrect. Allow listing restricts which programs run on the live OS. It does not stop someone reading the disk from a different machine.","Correct. FDE makes the contents unreadable without the key, even if the drive is moved to another machine.","Incorrect. A HIPS blocks malicious behavior while the OS is running and offers no protection when the drive is mounted elsewhere."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Field staff at remote sites must send corporate data from mobile devices. Which connection method is generally the most secure?",c:["Carrier cellular data tunneled via always-on VPN","Public hotspot Wi-Fi with HTTPS-only browsing","Customer site Wi-Fi using a shared WPA2 passphrase","Bluetooth tethering to an unmanaged personal laptop"],a:0,e:["Correct. Cellular links are authenticated and encrypted by the carrier, and a VPN adds end-to-end protection for corporate traffic.","Incorrect. Public hotspots are untrusted and open to rogue APs and interception; HTTPS covers only web traffic. It fits low-risk browsing.","Incorrect. Anyone with the passphrase can decrypt other clients' traffic. It would be acceptable with a VPN, but is weaker than a cellular link.","Incorrect. Bluetooth is short range and routes data through an unmanaged device. It fits personal accessories, not corporate data transfer."]},

// ---------- 4.2 Asset management (10) ----------
{o:"4.2",q:"During an incident, IT cannot determine who owns an affected server or where it is located. Which practice would have prevented this?",c:["Data classification scheme with sensitivity labels","Vulnerability scan schedule for all hosts","Business impact analysis of critical functions","Asset inventory with named owners and locations"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Classification labels data sensitivity and sets handling rules, but does not record ownership or physical location of systems.","Incorrect. Scheduled scans find weaknesses but do not record who owns a host. They depend on an inventory to know what to scan.","Incorrect. A BIA ranks the impact of outages on business functions. It fits continuity planning, not identifying system owners.","Correct. An accurate inventory with assigned owners and locations identifies the responsible party and the system quickly during an incident."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"An organization labels its information as Public, Internal, Confidential or Restricted. What is the main security purpose of these labels?",c:["Prioritizing patch order by CVSS severity score","Matching protection and access controls to sensitivity","Defining how long each record must be kept in storage","Determining which assets are in scope for a license audit"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Patch priority comes from vulnerability analysis, which also weighs exposure and asset value. Labels alone do not set it.","Correct. Classification determines which handling, access and encryption controls each asset needs.","Incorrect. Retention periods come from a retention policy driven by legal and regulatory requirements. Labels may inform it but do not set it.","Incorrect. License audits belong to software asset management and are based on installed software, not sensitivity."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A company is donating retired laptops to a local school. Which action must occur before the laptops leave the building?",c:["Wipe drives per NIST SP 800-88 and record the sanitization","Reimage with the corporate build and re-enroll each in MDM","Delete user profiles and run disk cleanup on each laptop","Reset the BIOS password and clear the TPM on each laptop"],a:0,e:["Correct. Data must be properly sanitized, with documentation, before equipment leaves organizational control.","Incorrect. Reimaging suits internal redeployment where the device stays under company control. It does not guarantee data removal for donation.","Incorrect. Deleted files and profiles remain recoverable with common tools. Cleanup is not sanitization.","Incorrect. Clearing the TPM can make an encrypted drive unreadable but is not documented sanitization, and unencrypted data would remain."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"Policy for top-secret media requires physical destruction because the SSDs are at end of life and will not be reused. Which method is appropriate?",c:["Degaussing the drives with a strong magnetic field","ATA Secure Erase or cryptographic erase","Multi-pass software overwrite of all blocks","Shredding the drives into small particles"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Degaussing disrupts magnetic fields and has no effect on SSD flash memory. It fits magnetic hard disks and tapes.","Incorrect. These are purge methods that allow reuse, but the policy requires destruction for top-secret media.","Incorrect. Overwriting is unreliable on SSDs because wear leveling leaves cells untouched. It suits magnetic disks that will be reused.","Correct. Physical destruction guarantees the flash chips cannot be read, which satisfies a destruction requirement for top-secret data."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A vendor shreds a company's old drives and supplies a document that lists each serial number and states the drives were destroyed. What is this document?",c:["Chain of custody log kept by the vendor","Certificate of destruction from the vendor","Statement of work signed with the vendor","Non-disclosure agreement with the vendor"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A chain of custody log records each handler of an item, such as evidence, and does not attest that destruction occurred.","Correct. A certificate of destruction is proof of proper disposal, listing the media destroyed, for audit and compliance.","Incorrect. An SOW defines the scope of a contracted service before it is performed. It does not prove completion.","Incorrect. An NDA binds the vendor to confidentiality but does not document what was destroyed."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"An administrator runs an ARP and ping sweep to list every device on the network and compares the results with the asset inventory, looking for unknown systems. What is this activity?",c:["Vulnerability scanning of discovered hosts","Configuration compliance scanning of hosts","Network discovery (asset enumeration)","Threat hunting for adversary activity"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Vulnerability scanning checks identified hosts for weaknesses. Here the goal is simply to find what exists.","Incorrect. Compliance scanning compares settings against a baseline for known assets, not locating unknown devices.","Correct. Discovery enumerates the devices present on the network, revealing shadow IT and rogue devices missing from the inventory.","Incorrect. Hunting proactively searches for signs of adversary activity. It fits looking for compromise, not building an inventory."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A company must keep financial records for seven years for regulatory reasons and then destroy them. Which document governs this?",c:["Data classification and handling policy","Acceptable use policy for employees","Data retention and destruction policy","Business continuity and recovery plan"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Classification sets sensitivity labels and handling requirements, not how long data must be kept.","Incorrect. An AUP governs how employees may use systems and does not set retention periods.","Correct. A retention policy defines how long each type of data is kept and when it must be destroyed.","Incorrect. A BCP covers continuing operations during disruptions. It fits recovery planning, not records lifecycle."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A company needs one person who is accountable for deciding who may access the HR database and for approving the risk of its use, rather than someone who performs backups and maintenance. Who should be assigned?",c:["Data owner from the HR department","Data custodian in the IT department","Data processor at an outside vendor","Data protection officer for privacy"],a:0,e:["Correct. The data owner is accountable for classification, access decisions and accepting risk for the data.","Incorrect. The custodian carries out technical tasks such as backups and access administration, but does not decide who gets access.","Incorrect. A processor handles data on behalf of the controller, often a third party. It does not own the access decisions.","Incorrect. A DPO advises on and monitors privacy compliance but is not accountable for approving access to the data."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"What does an accurate asset inventory primarily provide to a security team?",c:["A list of which known assets have exploitable weaknesses","Proof that deployed settings match the approved configuration","The period for which each asset's data must be retained","Current records of what exists and where it is located"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Identifying exploitable weaknesses is the job of vulnerability scanning, which uses the inventory as input.","Incorrect. Verifying configuration is baseline compliance scanning. It relies on an inventory but is a separate activity.","Incorrect. Retention periods are defined by a retention policy based on legal requirements, not by the inventory itself.","Correct. You cannot protect assets you do not know about, so an inventory is the foundation for scoping every other control."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"To reduce the risk of counterfeit or tampered hardware being introduced during acquisition, which procurement step is most important?",c:["Select the lowest-priced supplier listing the same part number","Buy through authorized channels and verify firmware on receipt","Add the devices to inventory after they enter production","Accept the vendor's default configuration to preserve warranty coverage"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The lowest price often comes from gray-market sellers, which raises the risk of counterfeit hardware.","Correct. Authorized sources and integrity checks directly address supply chain tampering and counterfeit parts.","Incorrect. Inventory should be updated at acquisition, and this does not address tampering in the supply chain.","Incorrect. Defaults are widely known and should be hardened. Warranty is a commercial concern, not a supply chain control."]},

// ---------- 4.3 Vulnerability management (20) ----------
{o:"4.3",q:"A vulnerability scan logs in to each host with an administrator account to check installed software versions and registry settings. What type of scan is this?",c:["Credentialed scan of the target hosts","Non-credentialed scan from the network","Agent-based scan using a local agent","Passive scan of observed traffic"],a:0,e:["Correct. Logging in lets the scanner inspect installed software and configuration directly, giving more accurate results and fewer false positives.","Incorrect. A non-credentialed scan sees only what is exposed over the network, like an outsider. It fits testing the external attack surface.","Incorrect. Agent-based scanning uses software installed on the host reporting back, rather than the scanner logging in remotely. It fits roaming devices.","Incorrect. Passive scanning observes network traffic without sending probes or logging in. It fits fragile environments where active probing is risky."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A scanner reports a critical remote code execution flaw in Apache Tomcat on a server. The analyst confirms Tomcat is not installed; the banner came from a load balancer. How is this result classified?",c:["False negative: present but not reported","True positive: present and reported","True negative: absent and not reported","False positive: reported but not present"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A false negative is a real vulnerability that the scanner failed to report. Here the scanner reported something that is not present.","Incorrect. A true positive is a correctly reported real vulnerability. The analyst confirmed the software is absent.","Incorrect. A true negative means the scanner correctly reported nothing. Here it reported a nonexistent finding.","Correct. The scanner reported a vulnerability that does not exist on the host, which is a false positive."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A penetration tester exploits an unpatched flaw on a server that the previous quarter's vulnerability scan never reported. How should the scan result be classified?",c:["False positive: reported but not present","False negative: present but not reported","True positive: present and reported","True negative: absent and not reported"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A false positive is a reported issue that does not exist. Here a real issue went unreported.","Correct. A real vulnerability that the scanner failed to report is a false negative, which is the riskier error because it creates false confidence.","Incorrect. A true positive would mean the scanner correctly reported the flaw. It did not.","Incorrect. A true negative means no flaw existed and none was reported. The flaw existed here."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which CVSS base metric describes whether an attacker must be on the network, adjacent network, local system or have physical access to exploit the flaw?",c:["Attack Complexity (AC) base metric","Privileges Required (PR) base metric","Attack Vector (AV) base metric","User Interaction (UI) base metric"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Complexity describes conditions outside the attacker's control that must exist, such as race conditions. It would fit questions about how hard exploitation is.","Incorrect. PR describes the access level the attacker needs before exploiting, such as none, low or high.","Correct. Attack Vector describes the context from which the vulnerability is exploited, from network to physical.","Incorrect. UI describes whether a victim must take an action, such as clicking a link. It is separate from where the attacker must be positioned."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"What does a CVE identifier such as CVE-2024-3094 provide?",c:["A numeric severity rating based on exploitability and impact","A classification of the underlying weakness type, such as improper input validation","A standardized identifier for a specific publicly disclosed vulnerability","A catalog of attacker tactics mapped to defensive detections"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. That describes CVSS, which scores severity, while a CVE ID only names the vulnerability.","Incorrect. That describes CWE, which categorizes weakness types rather than identifying individual vulnerabilities.","Correct. CVE gives each disclosed vulnerability a unique ID so tools and teams can refer to the same issue.","Incorrect. That describes MITRE ATT&CK, which models adversary behavior and does not name vulnerabilities."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A vulnerability has a CVSS score of 9.8 but affects an isolated lab system with no sensitive data and no route from the internet. What should drive its remediation priority?",c:["CVSS score weighed with exposure, asset criticality and exploitability","The highest CVSS base score first, because it standardizes severity","Whenever the vendor's monthly patch release cycle makes a fix available","The number of hosts affected, starting with the largest group"],a:0,e:["Correct. Prioritization weighs severity in context, so an isolated, low-value asset may rank below lower-scored flaws on exposed systems.","Incorrect. Base score alone ignores environment. Ranking only by score would put an isolated lab host ahead of exposed, valuable systems.","Incorrect. Vendor cadence determines when fixes become available, not how urgent they are for the organization.","Incorrect. Host count is one input, but it ignores exposure and asset value. A widely deployed low-risk flaw might outrank a critical exposed one."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A patch cannot be applied for 60 days because of an application dependency. In the meantime the team adds an IPS signature and restricts network access to the system. What are these measures?",c:["Corrective controls (post-incident restoration)","Directive controls (policy-based instructions)","Deterrent controls (discouraging attackers)","Compensating controls (alternative safeguards)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Corrective controls restore systems after an incident. Nothing has been exploited here, and the measures are alternatives to patching.","Incorrect. Directive controls are policies or procedures that instruct behavior. These are technical safeguards.","Incorrect. Deterrent controls discourage attackers through warnings or visible measures. An IPS signature and access limits actively reduce risk.","Correct. Compensating controls provide equivalent risk reduction when the primary control, here patching, cannot be implemented yet."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Management formally decides not to fix a low-risk vulnerability because remediation would cost more than the potential loss. The decision is documented with an owner and a review date. What is this?",c:["Risk avoidance: stop the risky activity","Risk acceptance: retain the risk knowingly","Risk transference: shift loss to a third party","Risk mitigation: reduce likelihood or impact"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Avoidance means ceasing the risky activity or removing the system. Here the system stays in service.","Correct. Knowingly accepting a risk, documented with an owner, is a valid response when the cost of mitigation exceeds the expected loss.","Incorrect. Transference shifts financial impact to another party, such as through insurance. Nothing was transferred here.","Incorrect. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact through controls. The team chose to do nothing about this item."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Immediately after a patch deployment, the team reruns the same scan against the same hosts. What is the purpose?",c:["Verify the remediation actually worked","Identify assets missing from the inventory","Rank remaining findings by exploitability","Document exceptions for items left unpatched"],a:0,e:["Correct. Rescanning validates that the vulnerability is gone and the patch was applied successfully.","Incorrect. Finding unknown assets is discovery, which uses broad network enumeration rather than rerunning a targeted scan.","Incorrect. Ranking is analysis and prioritization, performed on results before remediation decisions.","Incorrect. Exceptions are recorded in reporting after validation shows what remains open."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Analysts gather information about a target organization from public sources such as social media, DNS records and job postings, without interacting with its systems. What is this?",c:["Active reconnaissance of the target network","Social engineering pretexting by phone","Threat hunting inside the environment","Open-source intelligence (OSINT) collection"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Active reconnaissance sends probes such as port scans to the target. It would fit if systems were touched.","Incorrect. Pretexting manipulates people by posing as someone else to extract information, rather than collecting public data.","Incorrect. Threat hunting searches an organization's own environment for signs of compromise, not external information about a target.","Correct. Open-source intelligence collects publicly available information without touching the target."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A company pays outside researchers a reward for each validated vulnerability they report in its web applications, with the program open to anyone. What is this?",c:["Vulnerability disclosure program (VDP)","Bug bounty program for web applications","Penetration test engagement by a hired firm","Red team exercise against live defenses"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A VDP defines how to report flaws but generally offers no payment. The stem describes paid rewards.","Correct. A bug bounty rewards external researchers for validated findings, usually through a public or invited program.","Incorrect. A pentest is a contracted, scoped assessment by hired testers rather than a reward open to anyone.","Incorrect. A red team simulates a real adversary against detection and response under agreed rules, not rewarding independent reports."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which source provides threat intelligence shared among organizations in the same sector, such as financial services, on threats and vulnerabilities affecting that industry?",c:["Open-source indicator feed such as AlienVault OTX","Commercial threat intelligence subscription service","Information Sharing and Analysis Center (ISAC)","Government advisories published by CISA and partners"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Open feeds provide broad crowd-sourced indicators for everyone and are not limited to a sector's members.","Incorrect. A commercial feed is vendor-produced and sold to customers across industries rather than shared among peers in one sector.","Correct. ISACs are member groups that share sector-specific threat information and best practices.","Incorrect. Advisories cover broad national-level threats and vulnerabilities, not peer sharing within a single industry."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A developer's source code is analyzed without executing it to find insecure function calls and injection flaws. What is this technique?",c:["Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Software composition analysis","Static application security testing (SAST)","Interactive application security testing (IAST)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. DAST tests the running application from outside, sending requests and observing responses.","Incorrect. SCA inventories third-party components and their known vulnerabilities, not the organization's own code logic.","Correct. SAST examines source code or binaries at rest for flaws.","Incorrect. IAST uses instrumentation inside a running application during testing. The stem says the code is not executed."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A tool sends large volumes of random and malformed binary input to a compiled network service to see whether it crashes or behaves unexpectedly. What is this?",c:["Fuzz testing of the service","Static analysis of the source","Software composition analysis","Manual code review by peers"],a:0,e:["Correct. Fuzzing feeds unexpected input to a running program to expose input-handling bugs such as overflows and crashes.","Incorrect. Static analysis inspects code without running it. This test exercises the running service.","Incorrect. SCA checks libraries against vulnerability databases. It does not send input to a service.","Incorrect. A code review has people read source code for defects. No input is sent to a running program."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A development team continuously checks its third-party libraries against vulnerability databases and receives alerts when a new CVE affects a dependency. What is this practice?",c:["Static application security testing (SAST)","Fuzz testing of the application's inputs","Code signing validation of each package","Software composition analysis (SCA)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SAST analyzes the team's own source code for flaws, not third-party packages against CVE feeds.","Incorrect. Fuzzing sends malformed input to running software to find crashes. It does not track known library vulnerabilities.","Incorrect. Code signing proves a package's origin and integrity, but does not report new vulnerabilities in it.","Correct. SCA tracks third-party components, often through an SBOM, to catch newly disclosed vulnerabilities."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which environmental factor in vulnerability analysis describes whether the vulnerable asset is reachable, for example internet-facing versus internal-only?",c:["Asset criticality (business impact)","Exposure (attack surface visibility)","Exploit maturity (available exploit code)","Risk tolerance (acceptable risk level)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Criticality describes the business value or impact of losing the asset, not how reachable it is.","Correct. Exposure describes how reachable the asset is to potential attackers, and it raises or lowers priority.","Incorrect. Maturity describes whether working exploit code exists, such as proof of concept or active use in the wild.","Incorrect. Tolerance is the amount of risk the organization is willing to accept, which is a policy decision rather than a property of the asset."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which of the following is an example of dark web threat intelligence gathering?",c:["Monitoring criminal forums and marketplaces for leaked credentials","Subscribing to a vendor's STIX/TAXII feed of indicators of compromise","Reading a government advisory on actively exploited vulnerabilities","Reviewing a researcher's public blog post analyzing a malware family"],a:0,e:["Correct. Dark web monitoring watches underground forums and markets for stolen data or planned attacks aimed at the organization.","Incorrect. A structured feed supplies machine-readable indicators from a provider and is not itself dark web collection.","Incorrect. Advisories are public reporting from official sources. They are not obtained from underground sites.","Incorrect. A public blog is open-source intelligence, available on the regular web."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A legacy HVAC controller cannot be patched, so the network team places it in an isolated VLAN with strict firewall rules. How does segmentation help here?",c:["Encrypts traffic to the host so exploit payloads cannot be read in transit","Detects exploit attempts against the host by matching traffic signatures","Removes the vulnerable component from the host with a virtual patch","Restricting traffic to and from the host to contain any compromise"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Segmentation does not encrypt. Encryption protects confidentiality in transit and fits VPNs or TLS.","Incorrect. Signature matching describes an IDS or IPS. Segmentation limits paths but does not inspect traffic.","Incorrect. Virtual patching blocks exploit patterns at an inspection point, and it does not delete the vulnerable component either.","Correct. Segmentation contains the blast radius and acts as a compensating control when the flaw cannot be fixed."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A company buys a cyber insurance policy to cover financial losses that might result from vulnerabilities it has not been able to patch. Which risk response is this?",c:["Risk acceptance: retain the risk knowingly","Risk transference: shift loss to a third party","Risk mitigation: reduce likelihood or impact","Risk avoidance: stop the risky activity"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Acceptance means the organization bears the risk itself. Here the cost is shifted to an insurer.","Correct. Insurance shifts the financial impact of a loss to a third party.","Incorrect. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact through controls, while the vulnerabilities remain unpatched here.","Incorrect. Avoidance means stopping the risky activity altogether, and the company keeps operating the affected systems."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"In the vulnerability management lifecycle, which stage verifies that fixes worked and then communicates results, trends and open exceptions to leadership?",c:["Identification and discovery","Analysis and prioritization","Validation and reporting","Remediation and mitigation"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Discovery finds assets and weaknesses at the start of the lifecycle, before any fix exists to verify.","Incorrect. Analysis ranks findings by risk to decide what to fix first, and it occurs before remediation.","Correct. The final stage confirms remediation through rescans or audits and reports status to stakeholders.","Incorrect. Remediation applies the patches or compensating controls. Verifying and reporting come after it."]},

// ---------- 4.4 Alerting and monitoring (20) ----------
{o:"4.4",q:"Which tool collects logs from firewalls, servers and endpoints, correlates events across them, and alerts on suspicious patterns in near real time?",c:["SOAR (security orchestration, automation and response)","DLP (data loss prevention) system","SIEM (security information and event management)","NetFlow collector (traffic metadata)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. SOAR automates response through playbooks after alerts exist. It fits automating containment, not correlating raw logs.","Incorrect. DLP inspects content to prevent sensitive data leaving the organization and does not correlate security logs.","Correct. A SIEM aggregates, normalizes and correlates logs from many sources and generates alerts.","Incorrect. A flow collector records traffic metadata only and does not ingest logs from endpoints and servers."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"What is the main benefit of log aggregation?",c:["Centralizing logs from many sources for search and correlation","Hashing or signing each log entry so tampering can be detected","Discarding low-value events at the source to reduce storage","Enriching events with threat intelligence to assign severity"],a:0,e:["Correct. A single repository makes investigation and correlation across systems far easier.","Incorrect. That is log integrity protection. Aggregation gathers logs and does not by itself prove they are unaltered.","Incorrect. That is filtering, which can reduce volume but also risks losing evidence. Aggregation is about centralizing.","Incorrect. Enrichment adds context after collection. It is valuable but separate from the main benefit of centralization."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Analysts at a 24x7 SOC have started ignoring SIEM alerts because most prove benign. Which action best fixes the underlying problem?",c:["Route every alert to a managed security provider in the order received","Increase log retention so analysts can review more historical activity","Add more log sources to give each alert greater investigative context","Tune rules and thresholds to cut noise while keeping true detections"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Outsourcing triage moves the workload but does not reduce the noise or improve detection quality.","Incorrect. Longer retention helps investigations but adds data, and does nothing to reduce the number of low-value alerts.","Incorrect. Extra sources can improve context but typically generate additional alerts, worsening fatigue.","Correct. Alert fatigue results from noisy detections, so tuning makes alerts more accurate and actionable."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which standard allows tools to automate configuration compliance and vulnerability checks using common content formats such as XCCDF and OVAL?",c:["SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol)","SCAP (Security Content Automation Protocol)","STIX/TAXII (threat intelligence exchange)","Syslog (log message transport protocol)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. SNMP monitors and manages network devices. It does not define compliance content.","Correct. The Security Content Automation Protocol standardizes security content so tools can perform automated compliance and vulnerability checks.","Incorrect. STIX and TAXII exchange threat intelligence in a structured form. They do not define configuration checks.","Incorrect. Syslog transports log messages from devices and does not define compliance content."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which tool detects and blocks an employee emailing a spreadsheet of Social Security numbers to a personal webmail account, based on the content of the message?",c:["Data loss prevention (DLP) policy engine","Secure email gateway filtering spam and malware","NetFlow analyzer for traffic metadata","Vulnerability scanner for mail servers"],a:0,e:["Correct. DLP inspects content for patterns such as SSNs and can alert or block transfers.","Incorrect. Such a gateway blocks inbound spam and malicious attachments. It fits phishing defense but does not recognize sensitive data patterns.","Incorrect. NetFlow records traffic metadata such as source, destination and volume but cannot see message content.","Incorrect. A scanner finds weaknesses in systems and does not inspect outbound data."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A network team wants to know which hosts communicate, how much data moves and when, without capturing packet contents, to limit storage. Which source is best?",c:["Full packet capture on a span port","SNMP polling of interface counters","Syslog messages from the routers","NetFlow or IPFIX flow exports from routers"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Full capture stores payloads and demands far more storage. It fits deep forensic analysis of a specific event.","Incorrect. SNMP collects device counters and status, not host-to-host conversations.","Incorrect. Syslog carries event messages generated by devices and does not summarize network conversations.","Correct. Flow data provides conversation metadata at a fraction of the storage required for packet capture."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A management station needs to poll network devices for interface counters and also receive unsolicited event notifications from them. Which protocol is used?",c:["Syslog (event messages over UDP 514)","SNMP (network device management)","NetFlow (exported traffic flow records)","NTP (network clock synchronization)"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Syslog only pushes log messages and cannot be used to query device counters.","Correct. SNMP polls managed objects and receives traps; SNMPv3 should be used for authentication and encryption.","Incorrect. NetFlow exports traffic flow records and does not poll device status.","Incorrect. NTP synchronizes clocks and does not report device status."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"What does antivirus software do when it quarantines a detected file?",c:["Permanently removes it along with its related registry entries","Strips the malicious code and leaves a cleaned file in place","Isolates it where it cannot run, preserving it for review or restore","Blocks the process from starting but leaves the file where it is"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. That is deletion or removal, which is irreversible. Quarantine keeps the file.","Incorrect. That is disinfection or cleaning. It modifies the file rather than isolating it.","Correct. Quarantine isolates the file so it can be analyzed or restored if the detection was a false positive.","Incorrect. That is block or deny access. The file stays in its original location rather than moving to isolation."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Why does an organization archive security logs to long-term storage for 12 months after they leave the SIEM's online index?",c:["To enable real-time correlation across current events","To prove log integrity through hashing at ingestion","To support later investigations and compliance requirements","To reduce false positives by removing duplicate events"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Correlation depends on logs being searchable online, not on archives.","Incorrect. Integrity is protected by hashing or write-once storage and can exist without archiving.","Correct. Archives let teams look back at historical activity and satisfy retention obligations.","Incorrect. Deduplication and tuning reduce noise at the alerting stage. Archiving does neither."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A security team wants a holistic view of the health and security of servers, network devices and cloud services. Which monitoring should it prioritize?",c:["Infrastructure monitoring platform","Application performance monitoring tools","User behavior analytics (UEBA) tools","Data activity monitoring of databases"],a:0,e:["Correct. Infrastructure monitoring covers the foundational components, including servers, network gear and cloud resources.","Incorrect. APM focuses on application behavior and transactions and would miss underlying network and server health.","Incorrect. UEBA looks for anomalies in how accounts behave, not the health of infrastructure components.","Incorrect. Data monitoring tracks access to databases and files, which is narrower than a view of infrastructure."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which is a disadvantage of agentless NAC posture assessment compared with agent-based assessment?",c:["Higher deployment overhead on each endpoint device","Inability to assess unmanaged or guest devices","Greater endpoint resource use during checks","Less detailed endpoint configuration visibility"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Needing software on every device is an agent-based drawback, which agentless avoids.","Incorrect. Agentless checks are actually helpful for unmanaged and guest devices where agents cannot be installed.","Incorrect. Resource use on the endpoint is a concern with agents, since agentless checks run from the network.","Correct. Without an installed agent, checks see less about processes, patches and settings, though deployment is easier."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which statement best describes why vulnerability scanners are important?",c:["They block exploit attempts in real time at the network edge","They find and rate weaknesses so fixes can be prioritized","They monitor all traffic continuously to flag anomalies","They apply missing patches to vulnerable hosts automatically"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Blocking exploits is the role of an IPS or WAF.","Correct. Scanners detect and assess weaknesses, and remediation is a separate step.","Incorrect. Continuous traffic analysis is the role of IDS or NDR tools.","Incorrect. Patch management tools deploy updates; scanners only report findings."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which tool protects a WordPress site from SQL injection and cross-site scripting by inspecting HTTP requests, parameters and cookies?",c:["Web application firewall (WAF) appliance","Network IPS (intrusion prevention system)","Host-based IDS (intrusion detection)","Stateful firewall (layer 3/4 filtering)"],a:0,e:["Correct. A web application firewall understands HTTP and can filter application-layer attacks.","Incorrect. A network IPS inspects traffic at lower layers using general signatures and lacks deep understanding of web application parameters.","Incorrect. A HIDS watches a host for suspicious changes and detects rather than blocks web requests.","Incorrect. A stateful firewall tracks connections by address and port and does not inspect HTTP content."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"What is the purpose of a SIEM dashboard?",c:["Automating containment actions based on playbooks","Preserving log integrity with write-once storage","Normalizing raw logs from different vendors into one schema","Real-time visibility into metrics, alerts and trends"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. That describes SOAR, which orchestrates response actions.","Incorrect. Integrity depends on storage and hashing controls rather than a display.","Incorrect. Normalization is done by the SIEM's parsers before data is displayed.","Correct. Dashboards summarize security activity and posture so analysts can spot issues quickly."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A SOC has 24x7 coverage and written playbooks. Which approach to handling alerts is best?",c:["Batch alerts into a daily digest and review them in severity order","Triage and validate each alert, then escalate or remediate by playbook","Auto-remediate every high-severity alert without analyst validation","Escalate every alert to the incident commander before any triage occurs"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Waiting a day delays response to active threats. Digests fit low-priority informational events.","Correct. Following a defined triage, validation and response process ensures consistent handling.","Incorrect. Unvalidated automation can disrupt operations on false positives. SOAR automation fits well-understood, low-risk actions.","Incorrect. Escalating before triage overwhelms leadership and slows response. Escalation follows validation."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which security tool detects suspicious behavior on endpoints, such as process injection and credential dumping, and can isolate a host remotely?",c:["NDR (network detection and response)","Host-based firewall (port filtering)","EDR (endpoint detection and response)","Signature-based antivirus scanning"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. NDR analyzes network traffic for anomalies and does not see process behavior on hosts.","Incorrect. A host firewall filters connections and does not detect process-level techniques.","Correct. EDR monitors endpoint behavior and provides response actions such as isolation.","Incorrect. Signature AV detects known malware and generally misses behavior such as credential dumping with legitimate tools."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A company automatically compares system settings against CIS benchmarks every week to detect configuration drift from its hardened standard. Which activity is this?",c:["Credentialed vulnerability scanning for patches","Red team exercise simulating attackers","Automated configuration compliance scanning","Proactive threat hunting for intrusions"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. That checks for patch levels and CVEs rather than comparing settings against a benchmark.","Incorrect. A red team simulates an attacker to test detection and response, not routine settings checks.","Correct. Benchmark scanning checks settings against a standard and flags drift.","Incorrect. Hunting searches for adversaries already in the environment, not for configuration drift."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Why is time synchronization with NTP important for security monitoring?",c:["Consistent timestamps allow accurate correlation across systems","Signed timestamps ensure logs cannot be altered after collection","Identical clocks let the SIEM deduplicate events from different sources","A common time zone lets analysts schedule scans in off-hours"],a:0,e:["Correct. Without synchronized clocks, building a timeline across devices is unreliable.","Incorrect. NTP does not provide tamper protection. Log integrity needs hashing or signing.","Incorrect. Deduplication depends on event content and rules, not on clock sync.","Incorrect. Scheduling concerns local operations, while NTP's security value is event correlation."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which of the following is NOT a primary function of a SIEM?",c:["Retaining logs for compliance reporting","Correlating events across sources","Generating alerts on rule matches","Applying patches to monitored hosts"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Log retention and reporting are core SIEM functions.","Incorrect. Correlation is a defining SIEM function.","Incorrect. Alerting on rule matches is a core SIEM function.","Correct. Patching is performed by patch management tools. A SIEM collects, correlates and alerts."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which option best describes alert tuning?",c:["Enabling additional log sources to expand detection coverage across hosts","Adjusting thresholds to cut false positives while keeping true detections","Reordering the alert queue by asset value so critical systems come first","Disabling noisy rules entirely until the next scheduled quarterly review"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Adding sources broadens visibility but does not refine existing rules.","Correct. Tuning balances sensitivity against noise so alerts remain actionable.","Incorrect. That is alert prioritization, which affects the order of handling, not detection quality.","Incorrect. Disabling removes detection altogether. Tuning refines rules rather than switching them off."]}
]);
